Date: Thu, 04 Nov 1993 15:43:28 -0500
From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: Mathematica is fairly stupid... 

Nick Szabo says:
> Perry Metzger:
> > One mistake I think AI people make in general is
> > attempting to get computers to duplicate the highest functions the
> > human brain is capable of without getting it to do the lowest ones.
> 
> In other words, we need to make computers capable of trophic
> reactions and conditioning before we can get them to do
> arithmetic, logic, algorithms, math, accounting spreadsheets,
> filing, and the like.  I guess the computer industry is one big mistake.  

No, we would need to make computers capable of those things before we
can get them to UNDERSTAND all those things rather than just
mechanically operating them. I can condition a parrot to perform
simple arithmetic without any understanding of what it is doing. I
defy you to condition a parrot to come up with physics discoveries.

> One big mistake AI critics make in general is assuming we have to
> mimic human biology to make computers do things humans can do.

There are some things you can get computers to do that are very easy
-- you can get computers to add, for example. However, as soon as I am
shown a computer that can recognize salt shakers from photographs
taken without trick lighting and rectilinear placement and yet does
not "grok" salt shakers at any deep level, I'll accept your
statements. Thus far, I haven't seen ANY real successes from AI, so I
think that the burden of proof is on the AI people to demonstrate that
I'm wacko, not the other way around.

Perry

