Date: Thu, 4 Nov 93 13:48:28 -0800
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: No New Continents (was: Future Science)

    I have followed the future science thread with interest.

    I agree with Tim May that the history of geography may be
typical of other sciences.  There is perhaps more to say here,
though.  Most of my scientific training has to do with astronomy
and/or astrophysics, and it is pretty clear that astronomy has
gone through many "cartographic" periods, in which most of the
work involved encyclopedic observation and description of the
fine details of what's there -- my PhD thesis project was
cartographic -- yet these have been interleaved with periods of
enormous progress both in discovering new phenomena and in
modeling and understanding phenomena with details already
well-documented.  Thus here we have a science in which the
transition between "continent discovery" and "filling out
details" has been made several times in both directions.
    Both kinds of periods have been important for the field as a
whole.  One is particularly grateful to dedicated and careful
data-gatherers of yore when studying phenomena that are
intrinsically subtle (like the perihelion shift of Mercury), or
that require good statistics to detect (like measuring the mass
density in the galaxy by looking at the distribution of bright
giant stars above and below the plane), or that involve long,
slow changes (like much of stellar evolution), or that are
infrequently observed (like bright supernovae or star
formation).  Cartography in these areas has provided mill-grist
leading to, or allowing study of, major discoveries;
respectively general relativity, the presence and amount of
"missing mass" in the cosmos, and various topics in nuclear
physics and particle physics.
    These considerations make it difficult to conclude with
certainty that a science is in a permanent state of filling out
the details.  I doubt that Tim meant to suggest that any one
specific science was necessarily in such a state, but even it
turns out that most are, it is important not to replace the
distribution by its mean.

    It's interesting to think about where fundamental
discoveries might be "hiding", and what it might take to shake
them out.  (Of course, the best I can do is to speculate -- if I
had anything more substantial, I'd publish!)  Let me continue in
great part with astronomy, from familiarity.  Some historical
precedents emerge:
    (1) Better observational instrumentation often leads to
discovery.  The history of astronomy is replete with discoveries
made via better spatial and temporal resolution, better
light-gathering ability, and better ability to observe in
portions of the electromagnetic spectrum previously unuseable.
    We do not appear near any fundamental limits in spatial or
temporal resolution, or in light- (or whatever-) gathering
ability, and there are other kinds of entities one might like to
observe (various elementary particles, gravitational waves ...)
Thus my guess would be that improved instrumentation will
continue to lead to surprises.
    (2) Mathematical discovery often leads to improved
understanding.  The application of calculus to kinematics and
dynamics, by Newton _et_al._, is the textbook example here.  In
junior high school, I taught myself calculus from books because
I was interested in such problems; I still remember the thrill
when I realized how much simpler things looked from the vantage
point of better mathematics -- I felt like a falcon who had
labored to gain altitude and now was set to stoop on the hapless
prey!  I have no sense that mathematics is in any sense "done",
and even if it were, the current rapid increase in computational
power surely will continue to allow better application of the
mathematics we have and better description of the results.
    (3) Paradigm shifts often lead to better understanding.
Thus it is perfectly possible to model planetary motions with
arbitrary accuracy by adding up circular motions -- today we'd
use terms like "Fourier series" rather than "epicycles" -- but I
suspect that the shift to the idea that the ellipse, not the
circle, was the fundamental form of the motion, made it much
easier to develop the concepts of Newtonian gravitation and of
angular momentum.
    And in this context, I am reminded of a comment Feyman made
now and then when I was at CalTech -- he said words to the
effect that he wondered whether our familiarity with the present
way we do physics might not be blinding us to the possibility of
some entirely different, "better" way, that we might discover if
we threw out everything we knew and started over.  (Feynman's
comment was almost certainly funnier and wiser than my
reconstruction.)
    But of course, new paradigms are notoriously hard to
predict, or even to articulate, when one is still heavily under
the influence of their predecessors.

    Widening the scope of the discussion slightly, I sometimes
wonder if there are any generic kinds of physical laws that our
usual approach to doing physics might have overlooked.  Again,
the best I can do is conjecture, and I have only one conjecture
at present.
    Physics has usually adopted an analytic/synthetic approach
to the discovery and application of physical laws; that is,
given a complicated system to understand, what you do is take it
to pieces, discover the laws that govern each piece separately,
then add up (or whatever, with mutual interaction, etc.) the
behaviors of the pieces to obtain the behavior of the whole.
But suppose that doesn't always work.  In particular, suppose
that for some metric M of the "complexity" of a system S, there
is a physical phenomenon P for which:

    M(S) <  (a threshold) implies that P does not happen --
               the effect is identically zero, but
    M(S) >= (the threshold) implies that P does happen.

    If the size of the threshold were large compared to the
level to which you took things apart for analysis, then the
analytic/synthetic approach would not suffice for discovering
the laws that governed P.  (And there are often limits to
analytical ability, or to physical size, that affect the level
of dissection you must use.)
    I need some names; I will call phenomena of this sort
"nondissectable", and others "dissectable".  I hasten to add
that I have no candidate nondissectable phenomena in mind.
    Given that the analytic/synthetic approach seems to have
worked pretty well, I'm not sure how you would go about looking
for nondissectable phenomena.  You might reasonably argue that
you would want to know all the laws of dissectable phenomena
first, for if not, then when faced with unexplained behavior of
a complicated system, you could not rule out the possibility
that just a little more scrupulous and careful analysis and
synthesis would serve to demonstrate that it was dissectable.
But of course, in proportion as you had a complex-system
phenomenon that refused to yield to ever more scrupulous and
careful analysis, then you might have to consider ever more
seriously the possibility that you had discovered something
nondissectable.
    I suspect that most present physicists, when faced with
unexplained behavior of a complex system, would most often say
"We almost certainly know the basic equations that govern that
behavior, we just don't have enough computational power to model
their application in such messy circumstances."  That is, most
physicists would suspect that the analytical part has already
been done, but that the synthesis is impractical.  I myself
would bet money on arguments of that form.  But how interesting,
that every increase in computational power makes it that much
easier to do synthesis, and that much easier to test for
the presence of nondissectable phenomena.

                                    -- Jay Freeman

