Date: Thu, 4 Nov 93 18:39:01 PST
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Future Science

Michael Price asks good questions about GP's recapitulation of Kepler's
laws.  I'm afraid I overstated the case; only the Third law was
tackled.  The simple version of GP used here, symbolic regression, 
doesn't go in for fancy concepts, it just symbolically generates the 
equation that best fits the data.  There are other kinds of software 
that can learn geometric relationships like ellipses, and GP would 
likely improve and generalize such software, but like many other tasks 
that lies in the future. I should also note that the interests of the GP 
researchers who did this lie elsewhere than astronomy or physics; their 
goal in doing Kepler's Third was simply to demonstrate that symbolic 
regression is a superset of BACON, a task they accomplished in short order. 
BACON also found Boyle's, Ohm's, and Coulomb's laws from the appropriate
data, and these would be straightforward for symbolic regression.

Since the researchers weren't really into astronomy,
the framework is fairly naive.  The data given was (presumably 
modern measurements of) the mean distance and period of nine planets.  
To set up a symbolic regression we need the terminal set T, in this case 
just the dependent variable DIST, a function set, a reasonable guess of 
the functions needed in this kind of model,  F = {+,-,*,/,sqrt,sin,cos}, 
and a fitness function, which measures the error of the resulting equation
for the period of the planet against the measured period, for all
nine planets.  

Interestingly, it took only a single generation to evolve (in
LISP prefix, convenient for storing parse trees) the
close but incorrect equation Kepler published in 1608:

period = (* DIST DIST)

After a few more generations, the it found the Kepler's
third:

period = (sqrt (* DIST (* DIST DIST)))

On another run it found 

period = (* DIST (sqrt DIST))

And also some equivalent but less parsimonious stuff, filled with
a lot of genetic "glue".  For more complicated problems parsimony
needs to be an explicit part of the fitness function, and equation
simplifiers and pretty-printing programs are needed, so the scientist
doesn't have to wade through the genetic goop to find the evolved
gems.

In this case the "mean distance" is conditioned data, but the
symbolic regression for deriving mean distance from 3D astronomical
observations, or even the raw spherical-coordinate astronomical
observations, should be straightforward, either as a separate
regression task or evolving as part of an attempt to find relationships
between those data and the planetary periods.  We can introduce 
uncorrelated data, such as the diameter of planets, with linear degradation 
in GP's performance.  We can introduce gratuitous functions (in this 
case +,-,/,sin,cos) with much less than linear degradation in performance.
So the scientist needs to include enough primitives to construct
the right model, but can include a few gratuitous data sets and a large
number of extra functions if it's not clear what primitives are
needed for the model.

We can also use symbolic regression to do symbolic integration,
symbolic differentiation, solve differential and integral
equations, find inverse functions, general functional equations,
numeric roots of equations (special case where terminal set is
empty), sequence induction, etc.  We can find symbolic answers
to problems that are determinstically unsolvable and we normally
only expect numerical answers.

Finally, symbolic regression is only a subset of GP's capabilities.
GP in general can generate arbitrary Turing-complete models against
any kind of objective function.  In symbolic regression we restrict 
the model to mathematical equations and objective function is 
error against the data.  For more general "conceptual" scientific
models we will likely want to move beyond symbolic regression
to the more general-purpose GP evolving various kinds of data
structures, (algorithms, default heirarchies, non-monotonic logic, 
Dempster-Shafer/Bayesian, etc., or unconstrained, letting an appropriate
data structure emerge).  Even though it is but a small subset of
GP, symbolic regression is a straightforward task itself quite 
valuable for the discovery of scientific laws, a broad generalization
of today's statistical regression packages across a very wide range
of mathematical models.  Of course, it would be much more interesting 
to tackle problems of cutting-edge rather than historical importance, 
and ideas for good places to apply symbolic regression in modern physics 
are eagerly solicited.

Nick Szabo					szabo@netcom.com

